@@ -235,7 +235,7 @@ A feature whose State carries `continuity` closes in this order. Every step is f
 1. Start the close lifecycle: `close-coordinator.mjs plan-start --root <root> --lifecycle <id> --actor <name> --close-intent durable-stop`, then apply the returned action.
 2. Take it to checkpointed: `close-coordinator.mjs plan-transition ... --phase checkpointed`, apply the returned action.
 3. Make the Result a Continuity binding: `pipeline-state.mjs continuity-result-bootstrap-plan`, then run the returned `applyAction` (`continuity-result-bootstrap-apply ... --activate`). Its completion prints, while the package is stale, the pending `preview` with its `requiredAuthority` and the `reconcileCommand`, and for a plan that needs the PO also `signatureCommand`.
-   **Project WITH a session-authority catalogue (D231):** the direct `pipeline-state.mjs` call refuses (`SDC-AUTHORITY-UNAVAILABLE`) because the State write must be owner-bound. For exactly the four entries `continuity-result-bootstrap-plan`, `continuity-result-bootstrap-apply`, `continuity-result-close-plan` and `continuity-result-close-apply`, run `node <plugin-root>/scripts/continuity-owner-route.mjs run --root <root> --lifecycle <id> -- <entry> [<entry flags>]` from the repository root, after the lifecycle reached checkpointed (take the entry and flags from the printed `applyAction.argv` from the entry name onward). One call binds a fresh per-invocation owner (a `controller-<random>` descriptor, not the host session and not the hook-observed owner, which is unauthenticated), runs that one entry through the unchanged gates and releases. A call killed in between leaves a binding: the next call prints `COR-BINDING-HELD` naming `routine-repair.mjs run heal --root <root> --reason "<why>"`, which releases it. A State write moves the image, and the next owner is refused (`companion-historical-owner-witness-missing`) until another route changes it; `close-feature` (step 7) is NOT on this route yet and keeps the direct route or the attended close until the follow-up slice.
+   **Project WITH a session-authority catalogue (D231):** the direct `pipeline-state.mjs` call refuses (`SDC-AUTHORITY-UNAVAILABLE`) because the State write must be owner-bound. For exactly the four entries `continuity-result-bootstrap-plan`, `continuity-result-bootstrap-apply`, `continuity-result-close-plan` and `continuity-result-close-apply`, run `node <plugin-root>/scripts/continuity-owner-route.mjs run --root <root> --lifecycle <id> -- <entry> [<entry flags>]` from the repository root, after the lifecycle reached checkpointed (take the entry and flags from the printed `applyAction.argv` from the entry name onward). One call binds a fresh per-invocation owner (a `controller-<random>` descriptor, not the host session and not the hook-observed owner, which is unauthenticated), runs that one entry through the unchanged gates and releases. A call killed in between leaves a binding: the next call prints `COR-BINDING-HELD` naming `routine-repair.mjs run heal --root <root> --reason "<why>"`, which releases it. A State write moves the image, and the next owner is refused (`companion-historical-owner-witness-missing`) until another route changes it; `close-feature` (step 7) is NOT on this route: run the action `finish-feature` or the coordinator returns. Its coordinator binding admits the direct route in a catalogue project too, unless a State operation is in flight (`SDC-AUTHORITY-HELD`); a call without that binding keeps `SDC-AUTHORITY-UNAVAILABLE`.
    **Read `preview.requiredAuthority` first.** `"agent"` (the usual case, G20-38): the plan moves only the Result entry, the fence-proven digest and the authority lift of the one Result entry Continuity binds, so every other artifact keeps the digest the approved manifest pins and the approved package is unchanged. There is no `signatureCommand`, no step 4 and no PO signature: run step 5 yourself with `reconcileCommand` exactly as printed (it has no placeholders and no proof inputs) and show the receipt it prints, which carries the before and after digests as the readback. The ledger records it as an agent-run, result-only reconcile labelled `agent-result-only-reconcile-no-po-proof`, never as a PO approval. `"po"`: the plan also changes a non-result entry (or is a legacy Result repair), so steps 4 and 5 below stay the PO-signed route.
 4. Sign the reconcile (PO only, in the PO's own terminal, only when `requiredAuthority` is `"po"`): run `signatureCommand` exactly as printed. It is the `po-human-approval.mjs authorize-critical --kind feature-package-reconcile` line, and `signatureCommand.subjectSha256` is the digest it binds (`criticalActionSubjectSha256` over `{ manifest, planSha256, candidate }`). It is never executed by the pipeline. A new commit changes the candidate and with it the digest: take the command again from a fresh `continuity-result-bootstrap-plan`.
 5. Reconcile the package: run `reconcileCommand` (`pipeline-state.mjs feature-package-reconcile ...`); on the PO route after replacing its four placeholders. `<po-name>` is the PO's own name, supplied by the PO, never by the agent. `<proof-request>`, `<proof-authority>` and `<proof>` are the `requestPath`, `authorityPath` and `proofPath` the signing terminal printed.
